WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.638
Picture a small bakery: three people in the back, and one door.

2
00:00:06.638 --> 00:00:08.869
A salesman comes by with a brochure.

3
00:00:09.067 --> 00:00:10.871
Badge readers on every door.

4
00:00:10.871 --> 00:00:12.353
A guard on every floor.

5
00:00:12.353 --> 00:00:14.608
A control room that watches it all.

6
00:00:14.608 --> 00:00:19.440
The tower down the street runs on it, and its managers would never go back.

7
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:20.874
It's a fine system.

8
00:00:20.874 --> 00:00:25.501
But the bakery has one door, and everyone inside knows everyone else.

9
00:00:25.667 --> 00:00:30.793
What the brochure leaves out is the monthly bill, and the person who has to look after it all.

10
00:00:30.933 --> 00:00:37.467
The last two lessons were the brochure: a receptionist at every door, badges checked in every handshake.

11
00:00:37.467 --> 00:00:38.975
Today, the missing page.

12
00:00:38.975 --> 00:00:42.431
And then the bakery's question: do we need any of this?

13
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.042
The bill has five lines.

14
00:00:45.042 --> 00:00:48.113
Latency, because every proxy adds a hop.

15
00:00:48.113 --> 00:00:50.569
CPU and memory, for every proxy.

16
00:00:50.569 --> 00:00:51.260
Upgrades.

17
00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:55.942
Debugging, because a failed call now has more places to fail.

18
00:00:55.942 --> 00:00:56.787
And people.

19
00:00:56.933 --> 00:00:59.931
The first two we can measure in five minutes.

20
00:00:59.931 --> 00:01:00.531
So let's.

21
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.632
Take the receptionist lab from two lessons ago.

22
00:01:04.632 --> 00:01:09.086
Send 300 requests straight to the app, and 300 through Envoy.

23
00:01:09.086 --> 00:01:10.692
Same app, same laptop.

24
00:01:10.867 --> 00:01:13.917
How much does one hop add to the middle request?

25
00:01:13.917 --> 00:01:14.933
Pause and guess.

26
00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:22.064
Direct, the median was 1.46 ms.

27
00:01:22.064 --> 00:01:24.980
Through Envoy, 2.09 ms.

28
00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:27.263
About 0.6 ms more.

29
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:28.270
Tiny, right?

30
00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:33.344
And the idle Envoy, with its tiny config, holds about 64 MB of memory.

31
00:01:33.344 --> 00:01:35.591
These are one laptop's numbers.

32
00:01:35.591 --> 00:01:40.013
Yours will differ, which is exactly why you measure your own.

33
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:41.215
One more thing.

34
00:01:41.215 --> 00:01:46.357
A sidecar mesh puts a proxy at both ends, so every call crosses two of them.

35
00:01:47.267 --> 00:01:49.523
Now a made-up payments product.

36
00:01:49.523 --> 00:01:51.852
Eight services, three Pods each.

37
00:01:51.852 --> 00:01:53.089
Twenty-four Pods.

38
00:01:53.267 --> 00:01:56.788
Give every Pod a sidecar at 64 MB each.

39
00:01:56.788 --> 00:02:05.184
Before a single request is carried, the proxies need 1536 MB, about one and a half gigabytes.

40
00:02:05.333 --> 00:02:08.007
The bakery would be paying a guard per loaf.

41
00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.382
Other meshes share proxies instead.

42
00:02:10.382 --> 00:02:14.869
Let's put our twenty-four Pods on three nodes, the machines they run on.

43
00:02:15.067 --> 00:02:20.515
Cilium, a network plugin with a mesh built in, is often called sidecar-free.

44
00:02:20.515 --> 00:02:23.454
So where does an HTTP retry actually run?

45
00:02:23.454 --> 00:02:24.601
Pause and guess.

46
00:02:27.867 --> 00:02:34.389
The tempting answer: in eBPF, the small programs Cilium runs inside the Linux kernel.

47
00:02:34.389 --> 00:02:35.464
Let's be fair.

48
00:02:35.464 --> 00:02:40.068
eBPF really does move packets and balance connections there.

49
00:02:40.267 --> 00:02:42.142
But it doesn't read HTTP.

50
00:02:42.142 --> 00:02:46.343
Anything that needs HTTP runs in an Envoy, one per node.

51
00:02:46.343 --> 00:02:49.119
Sidecar-free doesn't mean proxy-free.

52
00:02:49.267 --> 00:02:59.370
Istio's ambient mode is built the same way: one small proxy per node, ztunnel, and an Envoy waypoint only where you ask for HTTP features.

53
00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:03.104
Three node proxies instead of twenty-four sidecars.

54
00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:10.761
The price of sharing: a noisy neighbor can slow your traffic, and a broken node proxy hurts every Pod on that node.

55
00:03:11.533 --> 00:03:15.098
Now the bakery's question, for our made-up product.

56
00:03:15.098 --> 00:03:16.776
The lab has a checklist.

57
00:03:16.776 --> 00:03:26.423
One team, two languages, NetworkPolicies, one Gateway, OpenTelemetry already, and no auditor asking for encryption on every internal call.

58
00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:27.967
Every box is ticked.

59
00:03:27.967 --> 00:03:30.018
So the answer is: no mesh yet.

60
00:03:30.018 --> 00:03:35.281
Write a note to look again when a second team, or a compliance rule, arrives.

61
00:03:35.467 --> 00:03:37.586
And remember what a mesh can't do.

62
00:03:37.586 --> 00:03:40.080
It won't speed up a slow database query.

63
00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.761
It only wraps proxies around the slow call.

64
00:03:43.533 --> 00:03:48.616
If the answer is yes, adopt it one namespace at a time, one step at a time.

65
00:03:48.616 --> 00:03:49.903
First only observe.

66
00:03:49.903 --> 00:03:52.343
You'll find calls nobody knew about.

67
00:03:52.533 --> 00:03:55.114
Then mutual TLS in permissive mode.

68
00:03:55.114 --> 00:03:58.136
Then strict, once every caller is meshed.

69
00:03:58.136 --> 00:03:59.537
Then authorization.

70
00:03:59.537 --> 00:04:01.307
Traffic rules come last.

71
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.598
One last line of the bill hides in the easy path.

72
00:04:05.598 --> 00:04:12.207
Linkerd, a lighter mesh, creates a root certificate when you install it, the trust anchor.

73
00:04:12.207 --> 00:04:14.116
It expires after 365 days.

74
00:04:14.267 --> 00:04:19.582
A team that takes the defaults and never looks again has a mesh that breaks a year later.

75
00:04:19.582 --> 00:04:22.330
A certificate you didn't choose still expires.

76
00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:23.524
Write down the date.

77
00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:25.773
So the moral of the story.

78
00:04:25.773 --> 00:04:28.254
A mesh is a fine system with a real bill.

79
00:04:28.254 --> 00:04:33.035
Before you buy it, write down the two or three problems you expect it to solve.

80
00:04:33.035 --> 00:04:38.179
Measure them before and after, and take the mesh out again if the numbers don't move.

81
00:04:38.333 --> 00:04:39.482
A quick check.

82
00:04:39.482 --> 00:04:44.242
Ten services, two Pods each, a 64 MB sidecar in every Pod.

83
00:04:44.242 --> 00:04:47.115
How much memory before any traffic?

84
00:04:47.115 --> 00:04:48.920
Pause and work it out.

85
00:04:52.133 --> 00:04:55.852
Twenty Pods times 64 MB: 1280 MB.

86
00:04:55.852 --> 00:04:59.571
And still two hops on every call.

87
00:04:59.733 --> 00:05:06.481
Next, we leave the open-source parts behind, and meet one commercial product that collects every signal at once.

88
00:05:06.481 --> 00:05:10.819
What do you gain when one tool sees everything, and what do you give up?
